Project Description:
This program is a digital implementation of a Chain of Custody (CHOC) system designed for digital forensics. It allows users to securely manage and track the history of digital evidence by storing all actions (add, checkout, checkin, remove, etc.) in a custom-built blockchain. The immutability and transparency of the blockchain ensure that the integrity of the digital evidence is preserved throughout its lifecycle.
Each action on the evidence (add, checkout, etc.) is encapsulated as a block, containing encrypted case and item identifiers, a timestamp, user information, and state changes. Blocks are linked via cryptographic hashes and stored in binary format in a file specified by the BCHOC_FILE_PATH environment variable.

Features:
- Initializes blockchain with a genesis block (`bchoc init`)
  This function determines if the blockchain file is present. If not, it creates a genesis block and initializes the blockchain. If the file exists, it verifies its presence.
- Adds new evidence to a case (`bchoc add`)
  add -c case_id -i item_id [-i item_id...] -g creator -p password:
  Adds an evidence item, or a list of items, to a case. Requires the name of the creator and their password to add successfully.
- Checks out/in evidence for analysis (`bchoc checkout`, `bchoc checkin`)
  checkout -i item_id -p password:
  After verifying the owner's password, it checks out an item for investigation and adds a CHECKEDOUT block.
  checkin -i item_id -p password:
  After verifying the owner's password, it checks the item back into custody and adds a CHECKEDIN block.
- Displays existing cases and items (`bchoc show cases`, `bchoc show items`)
  show cases:
  If authorized it lists all case IDs, case IDs encrypted if not authorized.
  show items -c case_id:
  List all the item IDs in a selected case
- Shows blockchain history of evidence (`bchoc show history`)
  show history [-c case_id] [-i item_id] [-n num_entries] [-r] -p password
  Shows the activity history of a given case with additional filter options for a certain item ID and/or number of entries. Output is encrypted unless authorized access. 
- Securely removes evidence (`bchoc remove`)
  remove -i item_id -y reason -p password:
  After verifying the owner's password and providing a reason for removal, it ensures the selected item is CHECKEDIN and removes it from the chain, adding a REMOVED block.   
- Validates blockchain integrity (`bchoc verify`)
  verify:
  Validates the blockchain by checking for the following: Genesis block, duplicate previous hashes, sequential linking, bad blocks and messages, and actions after removal.
- Provides item status summaries (`bchoc summary`)
  summary -c item_id
  Shows the total number of CHECKEDIN, CHECKEDOUT, and removed items in a case.

Data is encrypted using AES (ECB mode), and stored in strict binary format. Timestamps are stored in UTC. All access is protected via passwords mapped to environment variables. The program exits with a non-zero status on any error, following UNIX conventions.


